# Rate Limiter Learning Notes

## Algorithm Comparison

| Algorithm | Burst | Output Rate | Memory | Best For |
|-----------|-------|-------------|--------|----------|
| Token Bucket | ✅ Allows | Variable | O(1) | User-facing APIs |
| Leaky Bucket | ❌ Queues | Constant | O(1) | Backend protection |
| Sliding Window Log | ❌ No | Variable | O(n) | Accurate limiting |
| Sliding Window Counter | ❌ No | Variable | O(1) | Memory-efficient |

## Key Insight: Same Capacity ≠ Same Behavior

With capacity = 10 and rate = 10/sec:

Token Bucket:
- 10 requests arrive → All 10 allowed instantly
- Backend sees: SPIKE (can overload)
- Capacity means: "how much burst allowed"

Leaky Bucket:
- 10 requests arrive → Queued, released 1 per 100ms
- Backend sees: Constant rate (protected)
- Capacity means: "how much can queue before dropping"

## When to Use Each

Token Bucket:
- User-facing APIs (better UX allows bursts)
- AWS API Gateway, Stripe, GitHub API use this
- Good when occasional spikes are acceptable

Leaky Bucket:
- Protecting databases/backends from overload
- Video streaming, network traffic shaping
- When constant rate is critical

Sliding Window:
- Per-user/IP accurate rate limiting
- When precision matters more than memory

## Production Go Packages

1. golang.org/x/time/rate - Official, Token Bucket, single server
2. uber-go/ratelimit - Uber's Leaky Bucket, smooth output
3. go-redis/redis_rate - Distributed with Redis backend

## How Leaky Bucket Protects Systems

1. Traffic Shaping: Converts bursty → smooth output
2. Queue Buffer: Short bursts queued, long bursts dropped
3. Cascade Prevention: Backend receives predictable load
4. Resource Protection: Prevents connection/thread exhaustion

## My Implementation Changes

Changed Leaky Bucket model:
- Original: remaining = "space left in bucket"
- My version: remaining = "fill level" (more intuitive)
- Start empty (0), increment on Allow(), decrement on leak()
